From: Gilly <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: restart the NPU on a full chip reset

The L1 SER path stops the NPU (mt7996_mac_reset_work(), MT_MCU_CMD_STOP_DMA
branch), hands it the new rings with __mt7996_npu_hw_init() once DMA is back,
and keeps NAPI off the NPU-owned queues.  A full chip reset (WM/WA watchdog)
takes mt7996_mac_full_reset() -> mt7996_mac_restart() instead, which restarts
WED but has no NPU equivalent: the NPU is never stopped, and never learns the
ring and descriptor addresses recreated by the WFSYS reset.  Host TX also runs
through the NPU rings, so Wi-Fi stays dead until reboot.

There is a second, sharper problem in mt7996_mac_restart() itself.  Its two
NAPI loops only skip queues when WED is active:

	if (mtk_wed_device_active(&dev->mt76.mmio.wed) &&
	    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
		continue;

	if (mdev->q_rx[i].ndesc)
		napi_disable(&dev->mt76.napi[i]);

Under NPU, mt7996_npu_rx_queues_init() sets hwrro_mode, so the RRO queues are
allocated (ndesc != 0) but mt76_dma_init() skips them because they carry
MT_QFLAG_WED_RRO, and only MT_RXQ_NPU0/1 get a napi from
mt76_npu_rx_queue_init().  napi_disable() therefore runs on a napi_struct
that was never registered, whose ->dev is NULL, and netdev_lock(n->dev) in
napi_disable() dereferences it.  The L1 path already guards against this;
the full-reset path does not.

Mirror the L1 path: stop the NPU at the start of mt7996_mac_full_reset(),
apply the same queue skips to both NAPI loops in mt7996_mac_restart(), and
re-initialise the NPU after the WED restart, failing the restart on error so
mt7996_mac_full_reset() retries it instead of leaving host TX dead.

Not exercised on-device: full chip resets are rare.

Link: https://github.com/Gilly1970/Gemtek-W1700K-6.18
Signed-off-by: Gilly <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: airola <airola@example.org>
---
--- a/mt7996/mac.c
+++ b/mt7996/mac.c
@@ -2325,6 +2325,13 @@
 		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
 			continue;
 
+		if (mt76_npu_device_active(mdev) &&
+		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
+		if (mt76_queue_is_npu_txfree(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
 		if (mdev->q_rx[i].ndesc)
 			napi_disable(&dev->mt76.napi[i]);
 	}
@@ -2341,6 +2348,13 @@
 		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
 			continue;
 
+		if (mt76_npu_device_active(mdev) &&
+		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
+		if (mt76_queue_is_npu_txfree(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
 		if (mdev->q_rx[i].ndesc) {
 			napi_enable(&dev->mt76.napi[i]);
 			local_bh_disable();
@@ -2394,6 +2408,17 @@
 				     MT_INT_TX_RX_DONE_EXT);
 	}
 
+	/* The NPU still holds the ring and descriptor addresses from before
+	 * the reset. Hand it the new ones, as the L1 recovery path does. Host
+	 * TX runs through the NPU rings too, so treat a failure like any other
+	 * init step and let mt7996_mac_full_reset() retry the restart.
+	 */
+	ret = __mt7996_npu_hw_init(dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(mdev->dev, "NPU re-init after full reset failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto out;
+	}
+
 	/* set the necessary init items */
 	ret = mt7996_mcu_set_eeprom(dev);
 	if (ret)
@@ -2472,6 +2497,11 @@
 
 	dev->recovery.hw_full_reset = true;
 
+	/* Quiesce the NPU before the chip is reset, as the L1 recovery path
+	 * does. mt7996_mac_restart() hands it the new rings afterwards.
+	 */
+	mt7996_npu_hw_stop(dev);
+
 	set_bit(MT76_RESTART, &dev->mphy.state);
 	set_bit(MT76_MCU_RESET, &dev->mphy.state);
 	wake_up(&dev->mt76.mcu.wait);
